Long ago, when the world was young, the night sky was empty and black. The Sun ruled the day with blazing authority, but when she set beyond the horizon, darkness consumed everything. People huddled in fear, unable to see, wondering if morning would ever return.

In a small village lived Ayana, a weaver known throughout the land for her luminous silver thread. Each strand seemed to capture moonlight itself. One night, as shadows pressed close, Ayana heard the Sky weeping.

"Why do you cry?" she called upward.

"I am so lonely," the Sky replied. "The Sun abandons me each evening, and I have nothing but emptiness to offer the world."

Moved by compassion, Ayana took her finest silver thread and began to weave. She worked through the darkness, creating tiny knots of light, then tossed them skyward. Each knot stuck fast, glowing brilliantly.

Night after night, she wove and threw, until thousands of lights decorated the heavens. The Sky rejoiced, no longer lonely. The people celebrated, no longer afraid.

When Ayana finally rested, the grateful Sky transformed her into the brightest star of all, forever weaving new constellations to guide travelers home.

